{
  "title": "Test a less restrictive marker-free codebook against word structure and decoding cost",
  "program": "L3",
  "on_path": true,
  "hard": true,
  "statement": "A fixed mixed-radix codebook with nine optional binary glyph slots, an optional one-of-eight gallows slot, and zero, one or two occurrences of e has 13,823 nonempty reversible codewords without a compulsory end glyph. It permits repeated glyphs and never puts two gallows in a word. On public practice-training samples, however, only 1.05% of ZL and 0.95% of IT distinct paragraph-word types fit its fixed ordered grammar; 3.55% and 3.66% respectively contain multiple distinct gallows, which the model forbids. The model also produces roughly one-third repeated-glyph types versus about 55% in both witnesses. This specified toy fails as a literal explanation; it does not rule out richer codebooks.",
  "check": "Specify a more flexible reversible codebook before evaluating manuscript text, allowing repeated non-e symbols and measured multi-gallows exceptions without a compulsory terminator. Require exact round-trip, report the paid dictionary/key size and the fraction of words representable, and test the same frozen rules on held-out pages and multiple transcriptions. Separately test whether codeword boundaries remain decodable when spaces are removed; do not assume the dictionary is free or that grammar fit establishes plaintext recovery.",
  "method": "Nine binary ordered slots emit EVA-basic glyphs a,o,d,y,n,r,l,ch,sh. Between the first four and last five positions is one optional gallows slot from k,t,p,f,ckh,cth,cph,cfh, followed by an e slot of multiplicity 0, 1 or 2. The all-empty combination is excluded. This gives 2^9 × 3 × 9 − 1 = 13,823 distinct nonempty spellings. No glyph is required, and no end marker is appended; spaces delimit codewords. Verify all 13,823 concatenations segment back to the intended eva-basic glyph sequence and are distinct. Draw a seeded random permutation of codewords, assign each encountered plaintext type its next unused codeword, then invert the completed dictionary exactly. Use first 25,000 normalized tokens of each of four pinned historical works with seeds 17, 37, 73, and compare with first 25,000 certain ASCII paragraph tokens from public practice-training ZL and IT. Compare distinct-type and token distributions separately: glyph length, any repeated or adjacent repeated glyph, one or multiple gallows, last-glyph frequencies and membership in this exact fixed grammar. The codebook was specified for this pilot before examining these measurements but was not preregistered.",
  "boundary_control": "The complete code space includes a, o and ao, so a space-delimited sequence a o and the single codeword ao become identical when the space is removed. The scheme therefore does not decode a continuous unspaced stream; it relies on visible word separation even though no dedicated terminating glyph is used. In the exhaustive code space e is the most widely present glyph (9,216/13,823 words) and sh is the most frequent final glyph (6,912/13,823), so neither is universal.",
  "original_control": "Enumerating Stolfi’s original nine-bit marker construction gives 512 word types, with type-length counts choose(9,k−1), exactly as stated. The new code is a different, larger construction; its three-valued slot and optional gallows alter that length distribution.",
  "source": "Jorge Stolfi, On the VMS Word Length Distribution (2000), https://mail.voynich.nu/hist/stolfi/word-length-distr.html",
